      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-157100",
        "knowledge_point_id": "1acdfb7b-9e3d-5475-bb07-d5e2944294f2",
        "knowledge_point": "Using an Implicitly Defined Curve to Determine a Second Coordinate",
        "problem": "Consider the curve ${x}^{3}-4{y}^{2}-2y=2.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2$ on the curve at the point where $y=1,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.",
        "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-4{y}^{2}-2y & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}-4{y}^{2}-2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{y}^{2}+2y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4{y}^{2}+2y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (8y+2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2$ and $y=1.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (3{x}^{2})-2\\cdot 10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=1$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $y=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-4\\cdot {1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3}-6 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =8 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 3\\cdot {2}^{2}-20 & =0 \\\\ 12\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
