        "math_academy_id": "q-115951",
        "knowledge_point_id": "1acdfb7b-9e3d-5475-bb07-d5e2944294f2",
        "knowledge_point": "Using an Implicitly Defined Curve to Determine a Second Coordinate",
        "problem": "Consider the curve ${e}^{x}-{e}^{y}=y.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3$ on the curve at the point where $y=0,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.",
        "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-{e}^{y} & =y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{x}-{e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{x}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot ({e}^{y}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3$ and $y=0.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)\\cdot {e}^{x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot ({e}^{0}+1) \\\\ -3{e}^{x} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=0$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-{e}^{y} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x}-{e}^{0} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}-1 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{0} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -3 & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
