    {
      "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925",
      "id": "q-116650",
      "status": "supported",
      "reason": null,
      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-116650",
        "knowledge_point_id": "b2dbfe46-55f0-5d6a-b410-164f0aa2345f",
        "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Related Rate With a Circle: Word Problem",
        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/4fa141d5af6f23e2eaa1d416def8f84e21e48f775b5b195cbc8ed74e4bb64218.png)\n\nA circular iceberg is melting in such a way that the superficial circle is decreasing its area at a rate of $24\\pi \\,{\\text{km}}^{2}\\text{/year}.$ Calculate the rate at which the circumference of the iceberg is decreasing at the moment when the radius is  $6\\,\\text{km}.$",
        "worked_solution": "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=6,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-24\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=6\\,\\text{km},$ $C=2\\pi (6)=12\\pi \\text{ km}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-24\\pi $ and $C=12\\pi $, and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{12\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ -24\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{24\\pi }{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the circumference is decreasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\text{ km/year}.$",
