    {
      "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033",
      "id": "q-103781",
      "status": "supported",
      "reason": null,
      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-103781",
        "knowledge_point_id": "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429",
        "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem",
        "problem": "A hot air balloon that is launched vertically is observed by a child located on the ground $4\\,\\text{km}$ from the launch site. What is the vertical speed of the balloon at the instant when its distance from the child is $5\\,\\text{km}$ and this distance is increasing at a rate of $12\\,\\text{km/h}?$",
        "worked_solution": "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/40957ce27304474263d1d231a3a0df93193ac0461d8a7444cde3b12047d1a214.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the child and the launch site,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the launch site and the balloon,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the child and the balloon.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $z=5\\,\\text{km}$ and $x=4\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $y$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {4}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ y & =3\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=12\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- the distance between the child and the launch site ($x$) remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(4)(0)+2(3)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(5)(12) \\\\ 6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =120 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{120}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical speed of the hot air balloon is $20\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$",
